analysisWe need to read the code and produce an assert statement for the comment marked "@@@". The comment:

```java
// @@@ If the list is initially not empty, then the returned element is the last element of the list before the modification
```

We are in method removeLast() returning E. It does:

```
final Node<E> l = last;
if (l == null)
    throw new NoSuchElementException();
return unlinkLast(l);
```

Thus precondition: list initially not empty (i.e., old size > 0, or old last != null). Then the returned element equals the element that was last before modification. So we need an assert using \old and \result.

We need to use public observer methods only. Which observer methods exist? In this class we have:

- contains(Object o) (public)
- element()
- get(int index)
- getFirst()
- getLast()
- indexOf(Object o)
- lastIndexOf(Object o)
- peek()
- peekFirst()
- peekLast()
- size()
- isEmpty()

Also maybe we can use internal fields? Not allowed; only public observer methods. So to refer to old last element, we can use getLast() before removal. But after removal, getLast() will refer to new last element. So we need to capture old last element using \old(this.getLast()). However, note that after removal, if list becomes empty, getLast() may throw NoSuchElementException. But we only need to assert when initially not empty; after removal, list may become empty, but we still want to assert that returned element equals old last element. So we can use \old(this.getLast()) to get old last element. However, need to ensure that getLast() is defined (throws if empty). Since we have precondition that initially not empty, it's safe.

Thus assert: \old(!this.isEmpty()) => \result.equals(\old(this.getLast())) . However need to use .equals for objects. Since E could be any type, we can use .equals method. But \result may be null; .equals may cause NPE. Usually they use \result == null ? \old(this.getLast()) == null : \result.equals(\old(this.getLast())) . But typical spec uses .equals. In examples they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) . So we can follow that style: \old(!this.isEmpty()) => \result.equals(\old(this.getLast())) . Wait \old(!this.isEmpty()) is same as !\old(this.isEmpty()). Better: !\old(this.isEmpty()) => \result.equals(\old(this.getLast())).

Thus final assert:

assert !\old(this.isEmpty()) => \result.equals(\old(this.getLast()));

But need parentheses: assert (!\old(this.isEmpty())) => \result.equals(\old(this.getLast()));

Better include parentheses around implication? Not needed but okay.

